◘ In Java, Optional is a container object introduced in Java 8 to handle null values safely and avoid NullPointerException.

◘ It represents:
    • a value may be present
    • or may be empty

◘ Instead of returning null, methods can return an Optional.

◘ Creating Optional Objects
     ➊ Optional.of()
      ==============
         Optional<String> name = Optional.of("John");
     • Used when value is definitely NOT null.

    ◘ If null is passed:
      • Optional.of(null);
      • It throws: NullPointerException

    ➋ Optional.ofNullable()
      =====================
      • Used when value may be null.
      • Optional<String> name = Optional.ofNullable(null);
      ◘ This creates an empty Optional safely.

    ➌ Optional.empty()
      =================
      • Optional<String> empty = Optional.empty();





